[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 500,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 539,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70971,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 209,
        "headingId": 539,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Squamous cell carcinoma NOS (SCC-NOS) is an invasive keratinizing carcinoma without any special histological features, for which p16 immunohistochemistry and HPV testing are not available.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:55:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 501,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 540,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70972,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 209,
        "headingId": 540,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8070/3 Squamous cell carcinoma, NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:55:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 502,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 541,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70973,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 209,
        "headingId": 541,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2C81.0 Squamous cell carcinoma of penis</p>\r\n<p>2C83.0 Squamous cell carcinoma of scrotum</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:55:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 503,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 542,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70974,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 209,
        "headingId": 542,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> epidermoid carcinoma NOS.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:55:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 504,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 543,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70975,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 209,
        "headingId": 543,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:55:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 505,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 544,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70976,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 209,
        "headingId": 544,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The majority of these tumours arise on the mucosal squamous epithelium of the distal portion of the organ (glans, coronal sulcus); primary or exclusive involvement of the foreskin is rare {32518604}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:55:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 506,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 545,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70977,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 209,
        "headingId": 545,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The clinical features of SCC-NOS are similar to those described for the usual SCC type {25677263}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:55:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 507,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 546,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70978,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 209,
        "headingId": 546,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/36/191\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Tumours of the penis and scrotum: Introduction</em> .</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:55:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 508,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 547,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70979,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 209,
        "headingId": 547,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>SCC-NOS is an acceptable alternative diagnosis where p16 immunohistochemistry and HPV testing are not available.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:55:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 509,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 548,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70980,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 209,
        "headingId": 548,
        "headingText": "<p>Smoking, chronic inflammation, phimosis, and poor hygiene are contributory in most cases; approximately 10&ndash;25% of cases may be HPV-associated but lack histological, immunohistochemical, and molecular evidence to support the association {26076979}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2022-05-05T15:55:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 516,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 555,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70981,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 209,
        "headingId": 555,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Gross features are non-distinctive and like those of SCC of the usual type, with nodular, ulcerative to cauliflower-like masses {31698006}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:55:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 510,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 549,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70982,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 209,
        "headingId": 549,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The histological features are like those of a variably keratinizing SCC seen elsewhere. The differential diagnoses include pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia, in which a gross mass and microscopic stromal reaction to deeply penetrating squamous mucosa is absent. Urothelial carcinoma of the distal urethra is also in the differential because it may have variable keratinization. An antecedent history of urothelial carcinoma and/or carcinoma in&nbsp;situ of the urethra is important to confirm urothelial carcinoma of the distal urethra. Urothelial carcinoma is also positive for CK20, GATA3, and uroplakin-2. Metastatic SCC to the penis is also a consideration. In such cases, there is usually an absence of surface intraepithelial carcinoma, an antecedent history of carcinoma elsewhere, and the presence of tumour primarily in lymphovascular spaces {24535700}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:55:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 511,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 550,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70983,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 209,
        "headingId": 550,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:55:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 512,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 551,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70984,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 209,
        "headingId": 551,
        "headingText": "<p>See <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/36/191\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Tumours of the penis and scrotum: Introduction</em>&nbsp;.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2022-05-18T17:37:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 515,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 554,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70985,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 209,
        "headingId": 554,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> an invasive carcinoma; evidence of keratinization in most cases; no information on (and no testing available for) HPV status.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:55:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 513,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 552,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70986,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 209,
        "headingId": 552,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Staging is performed according to the eighth edition of the Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) TNM classification {29325693}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:55:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 514,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 553,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70987,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 209,
        "headingId": 553,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The most important prognostic factors are similar to those of SCC of the usual type. Although series specifically looking at SCC-NOS are not available, the prognostic factors probably include histological grade; anatomical level of infiltration (pathological stage); vascular, lymphatic, and perineural invasion; and inguinal lymph node metastasis {28097264}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T15:55:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]